00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.160
Hey this is Presh Talwalkar

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:09.000
A standard dice has the numbers 1 to 6 each appearing with the probability of 1/6

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:16.520
If the dice is modified, so the number 6 appears with half the frequency of the other numbers

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.180
What is the new probability of rolling a 6?

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.780
I want to mention, I came across this problem in a new book "Power-Up" by Matthew Lane

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.020
The book uses video games to discuss really interesting mathematical concepts

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.900
and I learned quite a few new things

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.640
I did receive a free advanced copy of the book for review

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.060
and it's really a great book! You should check it out. There's a link in the video description

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.660
So returning to the problem

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.660
Can you figure it out?

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.620
Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for the solution

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:12.659
In a standard dice, the probability of rolling the 6 Is equal to 1/6, which is the same as rolling any other number

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:19.379
In this loaded dice, we want the probability of 6 to equal half the probability of rolling any other number

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.040
So one way you might think about solving the problem is that you might simply decrease the probability in half

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:32.860
That is, you would say the probability of rolling a 6 will be 1/2 times 1/6, which equals 1/12

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.300
But there's a problem when you do this

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.920
If you add up all the other sides, which still have a probability of 1/6,

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.780
then the total probability will be too small

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.580
It will not add up to 1

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:54.880
You will actually get that the probability of these numbers will be equal to 11/12

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.620
So this is the wrong answer because we need the sum of all the probabilities to be equal to 1

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.680
But no worries there's a way to fix this

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:12.740
Since we need the probability to be 1, we can scale each term by 12/11

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.820
This is so that we have 11/12

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.760
being equal to 1 once we scale it

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.920
and if we scale all the other terms by the same factor, we'll get the correct probability

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.880
so 12/11 times 1/12 is equal to 1/11

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.700
and 12/11 times 1/6 is equal to 2/11

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.980
and when we sum these new probabilities,

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.780
we do in fact get that the total probability is equal to 1

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.260
so this is the correct answer

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.600
The probability of rolling a 6 will be equal to 1/11

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.540
but the lesson here is that you can't simply adjust one probability

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.340
if you decrease the probability of rolling a 6 you have to compensate

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.800
by adjusting all the other probabilities

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.960
There's another way you could've come up with this answer

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.180
Let's suppose P is equal to the probability of rolling a 6

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.780
We then have the probability of rolling any other number will be equal to 2P

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.060
Now we need the total probability of all the rolls to be equal to 1

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.360
so, we can algebraically add up these variables

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.100
We have one term of P and five terms of 2P to be equal to 1

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.940
This will be a total of 11P that's equal to 1, which means P is equal to 1/11

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.260
So we get to the same answer but it was done in an algebraic way

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.060
rather than scaling up the probabilities and adjusting

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.940
There's another way you could have solved this problem

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.360
You could have also thought about the odds of the rolls or the relative probabilities

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.460
So the odds of rolling a 6

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:57.740
will be 2 to 1 against rolling a 6. It'll be twice as likely to roll any other number than rolling the 6

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:04.920
Now in order to convert these odds into probabilities, what we do is we add up all of these numbers

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.900
We have one for the six and we have five terms of two

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.180
This will be equal to 11

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:19.500
In order to convert these odds into probabilities, we divide each of the relative odds by 11

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.240
so we again get to the same answer that the probability of rolling a 6 is equal to 1 divided by 11

